response:
 Since this is a pentagon, we can first find the area of a simpler shape that contains the pentagon and then subtract the area of the triangles that are not part of the pentagon. If we can subtract these two areas, we have the area of the pentagon. In this visualization, a large rectangle is clearly shown, along with two triangles. The length of the base of the purple triangle is 8 by the diagram giving the dimensions. We notice that the height is 8 inches as well. We recognize that there are 8 inches in a foot, so the area of this triangle is $8\times8=64$. 


[asy]
draw((0,0)--(8,0)--(8,18)--(2.5,20)--(0,12)--cycle);
label("8''",(1.3,16),NW);
label("6''",(5.2,19),NE);
label("18''",(8,9),E);
label("8''",(4,0),S);
label("12''",(0,6),W);
draw((1,0)--(1,1)--(0,1));
draw((7,0)--(7,1)--(8,1));
draw((0,0)--(0,18)--(8,18));
draw((0,3)--(8,3));
draw((0.2,3.2)--(7.8,16.8));
draw((5,3)--(5,12));
draw((4.2,5.2)--(1.8,14.8));
[/asy]

Adding on these areas, we get $8\times18+64=216+64=\boxed{280}$ square inches.



The picture is a little confusing, and I did a little bit of manipulation to simplify the question. So the area will be 280 - the other triangles. Looking at the triangle on the far right, we find that the top will be 8 by line including the 6 in it, since the other triangles above it are still 6. So the area of the rectangle is 8*18, plus the area of the first trinagle (4*6 by looking at the dark triangle). So 144+24=168. Subtracting this from 280, we get 112 as our answer

